{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":8059641,"items":[{"institution":[{"name":"bioRxiv"}],"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,15]],"date-time":"2026-01-15T15:32:58Z","timestamp":1768491178250,"version":"3.49.0"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2020,11,10]]},"group-title":"Synthetic Biology","reference-count":20,"publisher":"openRxiv","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2020,11,10]],"date-time":"2020-11-10T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1604966400000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by-nc-nd\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"accepted":{"date-parts":[[2020,11,10]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                <jats:p>Cellular signaling pathways are responsible for decision making that sustains life. Most signaling pathways include post-translational modification cycles, that process multiple inputs and are tightly interconnected. Here we consider a model for phosphorylation\/dephosphorylation cycles, and we show that under some assumptions they can operate as molecular neurons or perceptrons, that generate sigmoidal-like activation functions by processing sums of inputs with positive and negative weights. We carry out a steady-state and structural stability analysis for single molecular perceptrons as well as for feedforward interconnections, concluding that interconnected phosphorylation\/dephosphorylation cycles may work as multi-layer biomolecular neural networks (BNNs) with the capacity to perform a variety of computations. As an application, we design signaling networks that behave as linear and non-linear classifiers.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1101\/2020.11.10.377077","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2020,11,11]],"date-time":"2020-11-11T02:05:18Z","timestamp":1605060318000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":3,"title":["Signaling-based neural networks for cellular computation"]